window.VGRE_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url": "index.html", "title": "Introduction", "section": "Overview", "text": "VGRE — Virtual GPU RuntimeRun unmodified CUDA on any CPU — no GPU required. Plus a full local-AI stack: train, fine-tune with LoRA, retrieve with an in-tree vector index, and generate — all offline, all dependency-free.Linux CI-greenmacOS CI-greenWindows CI-greenLLVM-free buildCUDA ~95%Zero vendor lock-inVGRE intercepts CUDA and OpenCL API calls and executes kernels on the CPU. It does not need LLVM to run kernels: a from-scratch CUDA-C front-end (own lexer/parser → AST) feeds a four-tier CPU backend — a PTX interpreter, a compiled-closure tier with a cooperative fiber executor (__shared__ / _"}, {"url": "quickstart.html", "title": "Quick Start", "section": "Overview", "text": "Quick StartFrom a one-line install to a running CUDA kernel on CPU in minutes.Fastest: install the prebuilt wheel (no toolchain)Each GitHub Release ships a self-contained, LLVM-free wheel per platform (Linux / macOS / Windows). It bundles the native engine, so you need nothing but Python 3.8+ and NumPy — no compiler, no CUDA, no GPU:bashCopy# Use a virtual environment (modern Debian/Ubuntu block pip into the # system Python — PEP 668 &#x27;externally-managed&#x27;): python3 -m venv ~/.venvs/vgre &amp;&amp; source ~/.venvs/vgre/bin/activate pip install numpy pip install vgre-0.1.0-py3-none-linu"}, {"url": "downloads.html", "title": "Downloads", "section": "Overview", "text": "DownloadsPrebuilt, self-contained wheels — no compiler, no CUDA, no GPU.Every GitHub Release ships a self-contained, LLVM-free wheel per platform. Each bundles the native engine, so you need only Python 3.8+ and NumPy. The wheels are built and smoke-tested (import + train a language model) on Linux, macOS and Windows runners in CI.Latest release — v0.1.0Pick your platform, download the wheel, then copy the pip install command (each block has a Copy button):🐧 Linux x86-64⬇ Download vgre-0.1.0-py3-none-linux_x86_64.whlbashCopypip install vgre-0.1.0-py3-none-linux_x86_64.whl🍎 macOS (Apple Silicon"}, {"url": "installation.html", "title": "Installation", "section": "Overview", "text": "InstallationManual build steps and platform notes.TipThe quickest way to get running is the prebuilt wheel — see Downloads. Build from source when you want the dashboard, the cluster tools, or to hack on the engine.PrerequisitesLinux (Ubuntu / Debian)bashCopy# Full build (with the optional LLVM JIT): sudo apt install -y cmake ninja-build clang llvm-18-dev \\ libomp-dev libssl-dev libsqlite3-dev libkeyutils-dev zlib1g-dev # LLVM-free build needs no llvm-18-dev / libomp-dev — just clang + the above.macOS (Homebrew — paths discovered automatically at configure time)bashCopybrew install cmake llvm@"}, {"url": "system-requirements.html", "title": "System Requirements", "section": "Overview", "text": "System RequirementsWhat VGRE needs to build and run — and the optional libraries that unlock extra features.All platformsRequirementMinimumRecommendedOperating systemLinux 5.4 / Windows 10 22H2 / macOS 11Ubuntu 22.04 / Windows 11 / macOS 14CPUx86-64, 2 coresx86-64 with AVX-512 or ARM64, 8+ coresRAM4 GB16 GB+CMake3.183.25+LLVM / Clang16 (optional — only for the JIT build)18C++ standardC++17C++20Flutter SDK3.16 (dashboard only)3.24+PowerShell5.1 (Windows built-in)7+TipLLVM is optional. The zero-burden build (-DVGRE_ENABLE_JIT=OFF) needs nothing but a C++17 compiler and runs the full suite on the"}, {"url": "running-cuda.html", "title": "Running CUDA Apps", "section": "Overview", "text": "Running CUDA ApplicationsHow VGRE intercepts and executes CUDA on the CPU.Execution modelVGRE parses each CUDA-C kernel with its own from-scratch front-end (no Clang required) and runs it on the fastest of four bit-exact CPU tiers that accepts it: a PTX interpreter, a compiled-closure tier with a cooperative fiber executor, a hand-emitted native x86-64 JIT, and an optional SSA optimizing backend. When LLVM dev libraries are present, an optional Clang → LLVM IR → ORC-JIT path (-O3 -march=native) is available as the high-performance tier. Kernel grids run on an in-tree work-stealing thread pool "}, {"url": "cluster.html", "title": "Distributed Clusters", "section": "Overview", "text": "Distributed ClustersScale kernels across machines over LAN or WAN. Channels are authenticated with HMAC-SHA256, encrypted with AES-256-CTR, and compressed with LZ4.vgre-start commandsbashCopyvgre-start --master # start a master node (launches dashboard) vgre-start --worker # start a worker (LAN auto-discovery) vgre-start --worker --is-master # headless master (K8s / container mode) vgre-start --worker --master-ip &lt;IP&gt; # LAN: connect to a specific master IP vgre-start --worker --master-address &lt;HOST:PORT&gt; # WAN: hostname, IPv4, or IPv6 vgre-start --test # local self-test (master + w"}, {"url": "discovery.html", "title": "Public IP Discovery", "section": "Overview", "text": "Public IP Discoveryvgre-discover solves the WAN bootstrap problem — finding the master's real public IP and handing it to workers without manual configuration.CommandsbashCopyvgre-discover # show the real public IP + worker connect command vgre-discover --set-master # set VGRE_CLUSTER_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS for this session vgre-discover --register # register the master IP to a token-keyed KV store vgre-discover --find [BUCKET] # (worker) retrieve the master IP using the local token vgre-discover --unregister # remove the registration from the KV storeHow it worksPublic IP detection tries five HTT"}, {"url": "token-management.html", "title": "Token Management", "section": "Overview", "text": "Token Managementvgre-token manages the shared 256-bit cluster auth token — the secret that authenticates every node. It works from any directory on all three platforms.CommandsbashCopyvgre-token generate # generate a new secure 256-bit token vgre-token show # print the stored token value vgre-token fingerprint # print the SHA-256 fingerprint vgre-token set [TOKEN] # store a token pasted from another machine vgre-token verify # check the env var matches the stored file vgre-token copy # print the scp / robocopy command for workers vgre-token revoke # delete the stored token (with confirmation) "}, {"url": "dashboard.html", "title": "The Dashboard", "section": "Overview", "text": "The DashboardA real-time Flutter monitor for the running engine.Launch with vgre-dashboard. It loads the native library in-process and polls live telemetry — no mock data. Pages:PageWhat it showsDashboardLive utilization gauges, workload throughput, UVM activity, log stream.Kernel ExplorerJIT-compiled kernels, launch counts, occupancy, per-kernel timing.Cluster TopologyConnected nodes, per-node load, network heatmap.Hardware TuningToggle background compute, block-threads, profiler; set cache model.Memory AnalysisAllocation map, bandwidth stats, UVM page residency.SettingsPolling interval, data"}, {"url": "local-ai.html", "title": "Local AI: LoRA · RAG · LM", "section": "Overview", "text": "Local AI: LoRA · RAG · LMA complete offline machine-learning stack built into the runtime — no PyTorch, no FAISS, no external tokenizer library.LoRA fine-tuningvgre.nn.LoRALinear adds trainable low-rank adapters over a frozen base weight, so you can fine-tune on a CPU/laptop with a fraction of the parameters. Adapters merge back to the base for zero-overhead inference.pythonCopyfrom vgre import nn layer = nn.LoRALinear.from_linear(pretrained, r=8, alpha=16) opt = nn.AdamW(layer.adapter_parameters(), lr=1e-3) # trains A,B only # ... training loop ... layer.merge() # fold adapter into W for fast"}, {"url": "debugging.html", "title": "Debugging PTX (GDB)", "section": "Overview", "text": "Debugging PTX with GDBCUDA-GDB-style single-stepping through PTX kernels using a stock gdb — no GPU, no cuda-gdb install.How it worksVGRE ships a PTX single-step interpreter and a GDB remote-serial-protocol server. Each CUDA thread appears as a GDB thread (a lane); the kernel's own virtual PTX registers are exposed to info registers; and breakpoints, stepi, and memory inspection all behave normally in an unmodified gdb.Start the stubbashCopyvgre_ptx_gdbserver kernel.ptx saxpy 1 4 2000 \\ f32:2.0 buf:f32:4=1,2,3,4 buf:f32:4=10,20,30,40 u32:4 # prints the instruction listing, buffer addresses, an"}, {"url": "advanced.html", "title": "Advanced Features", "section": "Overview", "text": "Advanced FeaturesCache modelling, SIMD/AMX acceleration, Hopper PTX emulation, MPS, and the optional RDMA / gRPC transports.GPU L1 / L2 cache modelbashCopyexport VGRE_L1_CACHE_KB=64 # 16 | 32 | 64 | 128 export VGRE_L2_CACHE_MB=20 # 2 | 6 | 20 | 40cCopyvgre_cache_stats_t cs; vgre_get_cache_stats(&amp;cs); printf(&quot;L2 hit rate: %.1f%% hits=%llu misses=%llu\\n&quot;, cs.l2_hit_rate * 100.0, cs.l2_hits, cs.l2_misses);AMX + AVX-512 WMMA accelerationAMX path — 16×16×16 BF16 matrix tiles (Sapphire Rapids and newer).AVX-512 path — N=16 tiles, ~16× over scalar.Scalar fallback — always available.bash"}, {"url": "env-vars.html", "title": "Environment Variables", "section": "Overview", "text": "Environment VariablesWritten to ~/.vgre/env by install_local.sh and loaded automatically. On Windows they are set in User scope by vgre_sync.bat / vgre_env.ps1.CoreVariableDefaultDescriptionVGRE_LIB_PATHautoAbsolute path to libvgre.so / vgre.dll.LD_LIBRARY_PATHextendedDirectory containing the VGRE shared libraries.VGRE_LOG_LEVELINFOVerbosity: DEBUG | INFO | WARN | ERROR.VGRE_INSTALL_DIR~/.local/share/VGREInstallation directory.Execution backendVariableDefaultDescriptionVGRE_EXEC_BACKENDautoExecution tier: interp (PTX interpreter), cp (compiled-fiber), or ssa (Tier-2 SSA). Unset = fastest tier "}, {"url": "api.html", "title": "API Reference", "section": "Overview", "text": "API ReferencePython, C ABI, and the CUDA-runtime shim.Python bindingsThe vgre package wraps the native library via ctypes:SymbolPurposevgre.TokenizerBPE tokenizer; train, load_hf, encode, decode.vgre.LanguageModelIn-tree GPT; train, generate, save, load.vgre.nn.LoRALinearLow-rank adapters; adapter_parameters, merge.vgre.vector.VectorIndexHNSW ANN; add, search, save, load.C ABI (vgre_c_api.h)The stable C surface the wheel and dashboard bind to. Highlights:c++Copyvgre_init(); vgre_set_profiler_enabled(1); vgre_set_block_threads(0); vgre_get_memory_bandwidth_stats(&amp;stats); // tokenizer vgre_b"}, {"url": "cli.html", "title": "CLI Tools", "section": "Overview", "text": "CLI ToolsEvery command installed with VGRE, what it does, and how to use it.install_local.sh links the runtime tools into ~/.local/bin (add it to PATH — the installer does this for you). On Windows the same commands ship as .bat / .ps1 scripts (see the note at the bottom). Every command below prints usage with --help.Cluster &amp;amp; runtimeCommandWhat it doesCommon usagevgre-startStart a master or worker node (auto-sources ~/.vgre/env).vgre-start --master · --worker · --worker --master-ip 10.0.0.5 · --worker --port 7778 · --test (master+worker on one box)vgre-workerThe worker binary itself ("}, {"url": "production-status.html", "title": "Production Status", "section": "Overview", "text": "Production StatusEvery shipped capability and its maturity. \"Production\" means real CPU math with no runtime stubs, exercised by the CI test suite.FeatureStatusCUDA runtime intercept (cudart)ProductionLLVM JIT kernel compilationProductionFrom-scratch four-tier CPU backend (no LLVM)ProductioncuBLAS INT8 / FP32 / FP64ProductioncuDNN convolution (INT8, FP32)ProductionNCCL AllReduce (ring + barrier-tree)ProductionCUDA Graphs (IF / WHILE / SWITCH)ProductionVirtual memory (cuMemCreate / cuMemMap)ProductionExternal semaphores (eventfd / Win32)ProductionUVM managed memoryProductionNVTX profiling marke"}, {"url": "architecture.html", "title": "Architecture", "section": "Overview", "text": "ArchitectureHow the pieces fit together.LayersInterception — CUDA/OpenCL API shims capture calls at load time (a drop-in libcudart preload, or the C ABI / Python bindings).Front-end — a from-scratch CUDA-C lexer/parser lowers kernels to an AST (no Clang required); an optional Clang/LLVM path is used when LLVM dev libs are present.Execution (four CPU tiers, fastest-first, bit-exact) — see the table below.Memory — UVM via an OS page-fault handler; stream-ordered pools; LZ4-compressed cluster transfers.Distribution — authenticated, encrypted TCP transport; NCCL-style + software-RDMA collectives; "}, {"url": "troubleshooting.html", "title": "Troubleshooting", "section": "Overview", "text": "TroubleshootingFixes for the issues you are most likely to hit, by symptom.Dashboard fails to load (&quot;Failed to load native library&quot;)bashCopysource ~/.vgre/env ls -la ~/.local/share/VGRE/lib/libvgre.so ldd ~/.local/share/VGRE/lib/libvgre.so | grep &quot;not found&quot; sudo apt-get install libomp-dev # if libomp.so is missingvgre-start not recognised (Windows)powershellCopyTest-Path &quot;$env:LOCALAPPDATA\\VGRE\\scripts\\vgre-start.bat&quot; $env:PATH = &quot;$env:LOCALAPPDATA\\VGRE\\scripts;$env:PATH&quot; # if PATH not yet updated vgre-start --help .\\scripts\\vgre_sync.bat # reinstallWor"}, {"url": "faq.html", "title": "FAQ", "section": "Overview", "text": "Frequently Asked QuestionsDoes VGRE need a GPU?No. That is the point — kernels execute on the CPU. A GPU is never required.Is it as fast as a real GPU?No. Expect 10–50× slower for compute-bound kernels; memory-bound kernels are closer with AVX-512. VGRE targets learning, CI, development, and moderate workloads — not latency-critical production inference.Which platforms are supported?All three are CI-green. The full ctest suite passes on Linux x86-64 (399 tests with LLVM, 379 in the LLVM-free build — the required CI job), on macOS (Apple Silicon, auto-detected Homebrew llvm@18), and on Windows "}];
